  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard in Kibana to look at the panels. The rule was created with `enabled: true`, but I haven't waited for a run to confirm it executes.\n\n**Data:** `kibana_sample_data_logs` holds web access logs.\n- **Time:** `timestamp` is the time field.\n- **Traffic and client:** `bytes`, `response` (text, with a `response.keyword` subfield), `extension`, `clientip`, `host`, `request`, `url`, `referer`, `agent` and `machine.os`.\n- **Geography:** `geo.src`, `geo.dest` and `geo.coordinates`.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-c6fb9de3 Web logs overview\" (id `a1d86b99-3757-44fd-af8d-9ed5ad6137ff`), with a default time range of the last 7 days. It has four panels:\n- **Total requests:** a count metric.\n- **Average bytes:** an average of `bytes`.\n- **Requests over time:** a line chart of request count.\n- **Top response codes:** a data table of the ten most common codes.\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-c6fb9de3 5xx spike\" (id `cb06d2b0-3a8d-4721-977b-448dce7d44e0`) is an Elasticsearch query rule.\n- **Schedule:** it checks every minute.\n- **Condition:** it counts documents whose `response.keyword` starts with `5` over a 5-minute window. It fires when that count is greater than 10.\n- **Actions:** none are configured, so it will only show up as an alert in Kibana and won't notify anyone. Tell me if you want a connector attached.\n\nMy first attempt to create the dashboard hung because of a stray command in my own script. It never reached Kibana, so nothing was duplicated.",
